// The escaped install — a thunk minted UNDER an install and called with
// that install's extent long dead. It REFUSES, loudly, and that is the
// answer to a question this leg used to reserve.
//
// It pinned 7 for two years of sessions: the stateless-singleton tier
// direct-called the arm with an empty world chain, and the call was
// VALUE-SOUND by construction (the arm ignores __state — ponger answers
// its honest 7), so the compile gate grounded the demand by that measured
// licence. The text then said the eventual split — keep the licence, or
// refuse the dead extent by identity — belonged to install-identity, the
// modal crown's seat (PLAN §4③, band A).
//
// THE SPLIT IS ANSWERED, and not by band A. The two were never
// alternatives: the world bracket's CONTENT is the install's world, so an
// arm reached after its extent closed has no world to run under at all.
// Making the walk uniform (one perform node, one bracket) made the licence
// unconstructible rather than undesirable, so the conscious choice this leg
// was written to force is made here — the dead extent refuses.
//
// WHAT IS STILL BAND A'S: refusing at COMPILE time. The row cannot see a
// dead extent, and the executable gate clears Ping because ponger is
// installed somewhere, so this program compiles clean and refuses at the
// dispatch. Loud, not silent — nothing executes unproven — but a runtime
// verdict where identity would give a static one.
// Expected exit: 134 (the walk's refusal).

effect Ping { ping() -> Int }

handler ponger {
  ping() => resume(7),
}

fn make_pinger() = {
  let t = () => ping()
  t
} ~> ponger

fn main() = {
  let escaped = make_pinger()   // drift-audit: ignore — the BINDING is the fixture: the thunk must outlive the install's extent before the call
  escaped()
}
